// Now that methods have been covered in the previous document, we can look at dynamic arrays.

main :: () {
    // The dynamic array type is like this:
    //
    //     [..]Element_Type
    //
    xs: [..]s64
    defer xs.free() // Let us not worry about memory management

    xs.push(69)
    xs.push(420)
    xs.push(1337)
    println(xs)

    // A dynamic array is represented at runtime like this:
    //
    //     struct {
    //         data:     &Element_Type
    //         count:    s64
    //         capacity: s64
    //     }
    //
    println("sizeof(xs)  = \{sizeof(xs)}")
    println("xs.data     = \{xs.data}")
    println("xs.count    = \{xs.count}")
    println("xs.capacity = \{xs.capacity}")
    //
    // The underlying memory buffer (pointed to by the field 'data') is not grown every time a new
    // element is pushed. Rather the field 'capacity' keeps track of the size of the current memory
    // buffer, and whenever the number of elements (the field 'count') exceeds the capacity of the
    // buffer, it doubles the capacity and reallocates the buffer. Therefore pushing to a dynamic
    // array is O(1) amortized.

    // You can index into dynamic arrays.
    println(xs[0])

    // You can slice them.
    println(xs[1..2])

    // The ends of the slice will be inferred if ommitted.
    println(xs[..2])
    println(xs[1..])
    println(xs[..])

    // Bounds checks occur at runtime if '-O3' is not provided. The following lines will all panic
    // at runtime. You can try uncommenting them one by one to see the behaviour.
    // xs[4]
    // xs[6..7]
    // xs[42..0]
}

// That is all there is to know regarding the syntax and semantics of dynamic arrays. It's a simple
// concept really, nothing much to say.
//
// For practical purposes, it is the API regarding dynamic arrays that is relevant. For that go
// through the file "std/builtin/array.glos" to see the methods defined on dynamic arrays. You will
// notice the use of '$T' in that file. Don't worry about that, that is just polymorphism, which
// shall be covered in a later document.
